From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:32:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 26/26] close the descriptor of every frame no request carried

This reverses 0023's placement of the close, and it is safe now because
0025 stopped treating a dead descriptor as the device's verdict. 0023 was
written against a sticky import probe: present() closed the descriptor of
a frame whose wl_buffer was already cached, the next pull of that dma-buf
answered "fstat on DMA-BUF failed: Bad file descriptor (os error 9)", and
the streamer copied 3 MiB of NV12 per present for the rest of the run. So
0023 stopped closing those descriptors - and then the descriptors and the
capture objects behind them were never released at all. Measured on the
r0.53 driven session at 1280x720: /proc/PID/fd grew from 39 to 1024 (the
board's soft limit) in 901 presented frames, about 1.13 descriptors per
present, and each run that died that way left ~900 capture objects (about
1.2 GB at 720p) in the V4L2 pool, which is why dmesg's "dma alloc of
size" failures went from 15 to 45 in one day and even the 1382400-byte
720p request stopped being served. With 0025 in place, a descriptor that
the window closes costs one CPU-copied frame instead of the whole run, so
present() closes every frame that no request carried again - the frames
that reuse a cached wl_buffer included - and the dma-bufs are released as
the decoder drops them. The r0.51/r0.52 sessions that ran 577 s with zero
capture failures are what that looks like when the pool stays healthy.

Greppable markers: wayland-video fps, opennow-diag
---
--- a/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.cpp
+++ b/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.cpp
@@ -991,18 +991,15 @@
         return;
     }
     // Whether the descriptor went into a request: only then does libwayland
-    // own it. A frame that reuses a cached wl_buffer names the screen a second
-    // time without sending a request, and attachFrame decides that descriptor's
-    // fate itself, so only a frame that never reached the surface is closed here.
+    // own it. A frame that reuses a cached wl_buffer, or that is dropped
+    // before it reaches the surface, keeps its descriptor here.
     bool handedOver = false;
-    bool surfaceTookFrame = false;
     if (frame.fd >= 0 && frame.planeCount > 0 && frame.size.width() > 0
             && frame.size.height() > 0) {
         // Pointer positions are reported in surface pixels; the video size is
         // what they have to be expressed in.
         m_videoSize = frame.size;
         if (ensureSurface() && ensureDmabuf() && m_configured) {
-            surfaceTookFrame = true;
             handedOver = attachFrame(frame);
         } else {
             ++m_dropped;
@@ -1010,9 +1007,8 @@
     }
     if (frame.fd >= 0) {
         // A descriptor that went into a request belongs to libwayland now and
-        // is tracked by bufferFor(); one that never reached the surface at all
-        // is ours to close.
-        if (!surfaceTookFrame) ::close(frame.fd);
+        // is tracked by bufferFor(); one that never got there is ours to close.
+        if (!handedOver) ::close(frame.fd);
     }
 
     const quint64 now = monotonicNs();
@@ -1137,14 +1133,6 @@
     wl_buffer *buffer = bufferFor(frame, &queued);
     if (!buffer) {
         ++m_dropped;
-        // The descriptor reached neither an add request nor the surface's cache,
-        // so this process still owns it and nothing else will. Closing a frame
-        // that the surface *did* accept would revoke the descriptor the decoder
-        // holds for the same DMA-BUF: the next pull of that picture then answers
-        // "fstat on DMA-BUF failed: Bad file descriptor (os error 9)", which is
-        // how the device switches the whole run to copying three megabytes of
-        // NV12 per present instead of importing the DMA-BUF it was given.
-        if (frame.fd >= 0) ::close(frame.fd);
         return queued;
     }
     wl_surface_attach(m_surface, buffer, 0, 0);

